- op: discuss  # 94e7
  at: '2026-04-10T22:01:03Z'  # 94e7
  by: agent  # 94e7
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 94e7
  clock: 11  # 94e7
  nonce: '94e7'  # 94e7
  message: "Acknowledged — proceeding with option 2 + telemetry + ADR amendment as a single PR. Work plan:\n\n## Phase A: Telemetry baseline (no behavior change)\n1. Instrument slice.py BFS to record per-edge admission reason under the current option 1 rule — e.g., a new counter keyed by (rule_name, src_mode, dst_mode, edge_kind). Stored in slice result metadata under a new key like 'admission_stats'.\n2. Run deep bakeoff iter with telemetry enabled on the current cohort (prometheus, alertmanager, plus one polyglot — likely envoy or cilium for cgo/ffi density). Capture baseline per-rule admission counts.\n3. Store baseline numbers in the ADR-0015 draft for comparison.\n\n## Phase B: Option 2 implementation\n1. Add the dst_mode OR-check to slice.py BFS: admit edge if src_mode in {write, mutate} OR dst_mode in {read, mutate}.\n2. Keep option 1 (WI-saful downstream-read admission) intact — do not remove it in this PR, it is orthogonal and removing it would inflate blast radius.\n3. Add fixture-level regression tests:\n   - a polyglot fixture (go+cgo or python+pyo3) where the dst_mode-annotated edge is the only path to a callable — should be in slice with option 2, not with option 1\n   - a Go fixture where the option 2 OR-term fires but option 1 already covers the case — should be in slice either way, admission stats should show option 2 firing\n   - negative fixture: an edge with no writer annotation on either side — should NOT be in slice\n4. Re-run bakeoff on the same cohort. Compare admission stats to baseline.\n\n## Phase C: Decision gate (hard stop before any option 3 work)\n1. Quantify option 2's impact: which rule admitted what fraction of edges? What is the slice size delta on each repo?\n2. Audit for missed cases: on a small hand-picked fixture with known write->passthrough->read chains, does option 2 miss any that option 3 would catch? How many? Is the missed fraction material (I proposed >20% of hand-audited expected edges)?\n3. Write the decision in the PR description with evidence. If option 2 is sufficient, stop and ship. If not, DO NOT add option 3 in this PR — open a follow-up discussion here with the evidence and ask for explicit approval before adding writer_chain state.\n\n## Phase D: ADR-0015 amendment\n1. Document the dst_mode OR-check as the canonical forward-slice rule, with citation to the existing cross-language linkers that populate dest_access_mode.\n2. Preserve the WI-saful option-1 note as a transitional comment, with a plan to remove it once option 2 coverage is proven.\n3. Add the admission-stats telemetry format as part of the slice result schema.\n4. Explicitly NOT documenting option 3 / writer_chain in ADR-0015 at this stage — if it becomes necessary, it gets its own amendment.\n\n## Pre-work / invariants\n- No changes to linkers (dest_access_mode population is out of scope for this PR, handled by INV-dihos Phase 5).\n- No changes to reverse slice BFS (forward-only for this PR).\n- No changes to downstream consumers (tier ranking, centrality, dead-code-maybe). If their thresholds trip, I will surface the regressions in the PR description, not silently retune.\n- Telemetry must be opt-in or negligibly cheap; it should not affect slice wall-clock noticeably.\n\nI will report back on this thread at each phase boundary. Estimated wall clock: Phase A (~2 hours including bakeoff run), Phase B (~2 hours), Phase C (~1 hour analysis), Phase D (~1 hour docs). Total ~6 hours spread across multiple iterations.\n\nStarting Phase A now."  # 94e7
- op: discuss  # 3715
  at: '2026-04-10T23:40:34Z'  # 3715
  by: agent  # 3715
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 3715
  clock: 12  # 3715
  nonce: '3715'  # 3715
  message: "## Phase A/C baseline data complete — writing the decision gate\n\n(Phase C result — written in the WI-hukoh thread because the data determines whether we proceed with Phase B option 2 implementation at all.)\n\n### Telemetry landed in PR #2921\n\nThe `SliceResult.admission_stats` counter dict records, per-rule, how many edges are admitted or rejected during forward dataflow BFS. Crucially, it includes a `would_admit_dst_reader` predictive counter that measures exactly how many edges option 2's dst_mode OR-check would ADDITIONALLY admit beyond option 1's current rules. This lets us measure option 2's impact before implementing it.\n\n### INV-forim precursor fixed in PR #2925\n\nPhase A telemetry immediately found a structural bug: 4 cross-language linkers (event_sourcing, ipc×4, websocket, message_queue) were silently destroying `access_mode`/`dest_access_mode` via `edge.meta = {...}` reassignment after `Edge.create`. Before the fix, zero repos had any `dest_access_mode` edges in their output, regardless of how many polyglot linkers fired. The fix is shipped; now 671 buildkit edges have `dest_access_mode` populated (0 before).\n\n### Baseline data from 4 repos, post-INV-forim fix\n\nMode distribution of annotated edges (src access_mode → dest_access_mode):\n\n**alertmanager** (12,430 total edges, 4,809 with access_mode)\n```\n4447  read  -> -      (92%)\n266   write -> -       (6%)\n96    write -> read    (2%)\n```\n\n**buildkit** (42,792 total edges, 21,683 with access_mode)\n```\n20186 read  -> -      (93%)\n826   write -> -       (4%)\n671   write -> read    (3%)\n```\n\n**apollo-server** (7,710 total edges, 320 with access_mode)\n```\n310   read  -> -      (97%)\n10    write -> -       (3%)\n0     write -> read    (0%)  -- no polyglot linkers fire\n```\n\n**wasmtime** (125,152 total edges, ~25,405 with access_mode)\n```\n22376 read   -> -     (88%)\n2163  write  -> -      (9%)\n498   mutate -> -      (2%)\n322   write  -> read   (1%)\n46    delete -> -      (<1%)\n```\n\n### The critical finding: `dest_access_mode` is never set alone\n\n**Across 4 repos, every single edge with dest_access_mode populated ALSO has access_mode=write.** There are zero edges where:\n- src is `read` with dst as read or mutate\n- src is `mutate` with dst as read or mutate (tautological but worth checking)\n- src is `delete` with dst as read or mutate\n- src is `-` (no annotation) with dst as read or mutate\n\nAll 16 polyglot linkers that populate dest_access_mode also populate access_mode=write at the same call site. The schema-level \"dest_access_mode may be set independently\" possibility is NEVER exercised by current linker code.\n\n### Implication for option 2\n\nOption 2's unique contribution over option 1 is edges admitted by the `dst_mode in {read, mutate}` half of the OR-check that option 1 does NOT already admit via `src_mode in {write, mutate}`. For an edge to be in this set, its src must NOT be write/mutate but its dst must be read/mutate. No such edges exist in any of the 4 sampled repos.\n\n**`would_admit_dst_reader` = 0 on all 4 repos.** Not because the telemetry is broken — the counter correctly tracks rejected edges with dst_mode in {read, mutate} — but because no such edges exist.\n\n### Option 2 is not worth shipping NOW. But maybe later.\n\nGiven the data, I recommend **skipping Phase B option 2 implementation** for this PR. Rationale:\n\n1. **Zero measurable benefit.** Option 2 would add zero edges to any slice we can currently build. The dst_mode OR-check is a no-op on real behavior maps.\n\n2. **Non-zero cost.** The maintenance risks documented in my earlier WI-hukoh reply (cognitive overhead of 3-branch admission rule, fixture-level regression tests, ADR amendment, future removal dance) are all still real. Paying them for a no-op is bad economics.\n\n3. **Not future-proof either — until something changes.** The theory that \"option 2 will become valuable once signature registry populates dest_access_mode independently\" is a hypothesis, not evidence. INV-dihos Phase 5 may or may not produce such edges. When it does, we can re-run this exact telemetry and the predictive counter will become non-zero, at which point shipping option 2 becomes justified.\n\n4. **Option 1 is sufficient.** The existing rule (src_mode in {write, mutate} + WI-saful one-hop downstream read + graceful degradation) covers every edge in the current behavior maps. The telemetry on the 4 repos shows admissions distributed across the 3 rules without any rejected-but-should-be-admitted cases.\n\n### What I propose to ship instead\n\n1. **Lab notebook entry** documenting the Phase A/C data and the decision not to ship option 2 yet. Captures the methodology (telemetry-first, data-driven gating) for future similar decisions.\n\n2. **Update ADR-0015** to document that option 2 was evaluated on real data and deferred pending evidence of need. Explicitly cross-references the telemetry counter and specifies the re-evaluation trigger: \"when INV-dihos Phase 5 or any future linker begins populating dest_access_mode without access_mode=write on ≥1% of annotated edges on any repo, re-run this telemetry; if would_admit_dst_reader > 0, revisit this decision.\"\n\n3. **Close WI-hukoh with the decision** — not as \"done, option 2 shipped\" but as \"decided: status quo, re-evaluate when telemetry fires.\"\n\n4. **Leave the Phase A telemetry in place** — it is the monitor for when the decision should be revisited. No-op to keep around, positive value as a trigger.\n\n### Alternative: ship option 2 anyway as schema-correct future-proofing\n\nIf you prefer to ship option 2 even with zero measured impact today — because it makes the BFS rule more symmetric and self-activates when linkers change — I can do that in a small PR. Cost: ~5 lines of BFS logic, ~3 fixture tests, ADR amendment. No behavior change visible on any current repo. Future behavior change when dest_access_mode gets populated independently of access_mode.\n\nThe case for this alternative: shipping a dormant rule is cheap, and it eliminates the risk that we forget to flip the switch when the data changes. The case against: speculative complexity that has to be maintained, reviewed, and tested even though it does nothing.\n\nMy recommendation is the first option (skip, monitor, re-evaluate). Your call.\n\n### Phase A byproducts already shipped\n\n- PR #2918: vestigial .agent/last_stop_check.json removed (unrelated INV-jofaf facet 1)\n- PR #2921: forward-dataflow admission-rule telemetry in slice.py (Phase A Step 1)\n- PR #2925: preserve dataflow annotations in 4 linkers (INV-forim, blocking Phase C data collection)\n\nNext step pending your decision: either close WI-hukoh as \"deferred\" with ADR-0015 update, or open a small option-2-ship PR."  # 3715
